    "rationale": "Evidence: the iteration-0 design (base_speed 1.8, sensor y 0.06, sensor_size 0.016, kp 2.5, kd 0.2, ki 0.3) ran 60 trials and ended off track every time, with progress 0.11 to 0.17. rms_error stayed low (0.02 to 0.03 m) and max_error sat at the 0.06 m off-track limit, with one line-loss event per run. That pattern points to a discrete loss, not gradual drift. The most likely mechanism is that the robot reaches about 0.05 m offset during a bend, the outer sensors lose the tape, and the controller cannot recover. The current sensor row spans only x = ±0.04 (footprint edge ±0.048), so the tape is invisible beyond roughly 0.06 m offset, which is the same as the off-track limit.\n\nAccepted from the critique: (1) the 3 s limit needs about 1.5 m/s average, so I will not drop speed to 1.2 as the draft did. The iteration-0 race showed that 1.8 was not the cause of the early loss, so lowering speed is not the fix, and I keep base_speed at 1.5 to protect the completion bonus. (2) The critique's point that the race changes many things at once is valid, so I will track which change addresses which failure. (3) The sensor row is too narrow to see the tape at the offsets where the robot fails, which is the main new change.\n\nChanges from iteration 0: (a) Sensor x spread widens to -0.08, -0.04, 0, 0.04, 0.08. This keeps the tape visible out to about 0.09 m, beyond the off-track limit, so the controller gets a readable signal during recovery. (b) With a pitch of 0.04 m and 16 mm footprints, the gap is 24 mm, slightly less than the 25 mm tape, so the tape is always read by at least one sensor. (c) The wider spread roughly halves the signal change per metre of offset, so kp rises from 2.5 to 3.5 to keep the correction strength near its previous level (not doubled, to limit oscillation). (d) kd rises from 0.2 to 0.4 to damp the faster bend corrections. (e) Sensor y stays at 0.06 for lead. (f) ki stays at 0.3 to counter the fixed motor mismatch, which the critique identified as a source of bias.\n\nRejected: I did not widen body_width, because the off-track limit stays at 0.06 m for any body_width below 0.19. I did not lower base_speed to 1.2, because completion needs speed and the iteration-0 evidence does not link the failure to speed.\n\nExpected result: fewer line-loss events, and max_error below 0.06 on the s-bend and slalom. If the robot still leaves the track at about 0.05 m with the tape visible, the next revision should test kp and kd, not sensor geometry. If it completes with completion_time above 2.9 s, raise base_speed. If steering_oscillation rises above about 0.15, lower kd.",
